course: dictionary
lesson: 10849
title: Very, the word that makes words stronger
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Very» is a small word that makes another word stronger. Very big means more than just big, and very small means smaller than just small. «Walking sharks can survive for hours with very little oxygen when a rock pool gets warm and stale at low tide.» Here very makes little stronger: there is only a tiny amount of oxygen.
explain: Very can also mean exactly this one, as in «the very first», «the very end», or «the very same book». Then it points to the exact one, such as the first of all or the last part. It does not make the word stronger.
explain: Not very means only a little. «Young Portly hasn't learnt to swim very well yet» tells you he is not good at swimming yet. When you write, you can try a stronger word instead of very: huge for very big, tiny for very small.
fact: Very is an adverb when it makes an adjective or another adverb stronger: very big, very well.
fact: As an adjective, very means exactly or just this one: the very same book, at the very top.
fact: Not very means only a little: not very cold.
fact: Phrases with very include very much, very good, and at the very least.
fact: «Very rarely» makes rarely stronger, so money lost in the street is «very rarely found again» means it is hardly ever found.
exercise: «Walking sharks can survive for hours with very little oxygen.» What does very do to little? | It means the oxygen is lost | *It makes little stronger, so there is only a tiny amount of oxygen | It means there is a huge amount of oxygen | It gives the oxygen a number
exercise: Which word could replace very in «very big»? | Small | Quiet | *Huge | Slowly
exercise: In «the very first day of school», what does very mean? | Not at all | A number, like one | Very happy and excited | *The first of all
exercise: «Not very cold» means which of these? | *Only a little cold | Warm and sunny | Not cold at all | Extremely freezing
exercise: Which phrase uses very to mean exactly this one? | Very much thanks | Very good at maths | *At the very top | Very happy
test: In «young Portly hasn't learnt to swim very well yet», what does very do to well? | It means he has swum far away | It turns well into a number | *It makes well stronger, so he is not good at swimming yet | It means he swims like a fish
test: Which of these is the best stronger word to use instead of «very tiny»? | Large | Somewhat | *Minuscule | Very very
exam: What does very do in «at the very least»? | It turns least into a number | *It points to the exact smallest amount, the bare minimum | It means at the very most | It makes least stronger, so it means the least you can do
